1
00:00:31,651 --> 00:00:35,653
Both poles of our
planet are covered with ice

2
00:00:36,014 --> 00:00:40,817
They're the largest and most demanding wildernesses of all

3
00:00:42,779 --> 00:00:47,622
Nowhere else on
Earth is seasonal change so extreme

4
00:00:47,942 --> 00:00:52,865
It causes the ice
to advance and retreat every year

5
00:00:54,307 --> 00:00:58,829
And all life here
is governed by that

6
00:01:17,112 --> 00:01:20,834
When the first
polar explorers headed south

7
00:01:20,915 --> 00:01:24,238
Giant cathedrals
of ice marked their entry

8
00:01:24,278 --> 00:01:26,799
Into uncharted territory

9
00:01:31,405 --> 00:01:33,486
Passing the
towering spires

10
00:01:33,526 --> 00:01:37,566
They must have
wondered what unearthly sights lay in store

11
00:01:42,447 --> 00:01:43,767
As they battled on

12
00:01:43,807 --> 00:01:46,688
The ice became
increasingly dominant

13
00:01:46,728 --> 00:01:48,928
But nothing could
have prepared them

14
00:01:48,968 --> 00:01:53,408
For the ice world
that finally loomed into view

15
00:01:59,019 --> 00:02:01,300
Terra incognita

16
00:02:01,460 --> 00:02:03,501
the unknown land

17
00:02:16,187 --> 00:02:19,068
At the southernmost extreme of our planet

18
00:02:19,108 --> 00:02:24,710
the continent of Antarctica is as large as the United States of America

19
00:02:32,106 --> 00:02:35,269
90% of all the
world's ice is found here

20
00:02:38,354 --> 00:02:42,357
This frozen world
is largely deserted

21
00:02:42,518 --> 00:02:45,481
until the
start of spring

22
00:02:50,047 --> 00:02:51,969
A daily penguins

23
00:02:53,011 --> 00:02:54,291
In a hurry

24
00:03:00,692 --> 00:03:02,772
The clock
is ticking

25
00:03:02,893 --> 00:03:05,293
Instead of
waiting for the summer melt

26
00:03:05,333 --> 00:03:09,134
The new arrivals
hasten south over the frozen sea

27
00:03:21,696 --> 00:03:23,816
They have come
here to breed

28
00:03:23,975 --> 00:03:26,415
But polar
summers are so short

29
00:03:26,455 --> 00:03:30,494
They must be in
position before the thaw starts

30
00:03:35,733 --> 00:03:37,693
As the sea
ice retreats

31
00:03:37,733 --> 00:03:40,492
life can
journey farther south

32
00:03:46,986 --> 00:03:49,227
Antarctic
waters are so rich

33
00:03:49,267 --> 00:03:52,109
that visitors
come from far and wide

34
00:03:52,149 --> 00:03:53,709
to harvest them

35
00:04:01,715 --> 00:04:05,597
Vast numbers of
chinstrap penguins come ashore

36
00:04:05,637 --> 00:04:06,797
to breed

37
00:04:15,477 --> 00:04:19,638
No bird will lay
their eggs directly onto ice

38
00:04:19,678 --> 00:04:23,160
So bare rock is
a vital commodity

39
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:26,361
The best patches
are worth the climb

40
00:04:31,403 --> 00:04:34,204
The cliff tops
are soon stained pink

41
00:04:34,244 --> 00:04:38,685
With the droppings of tens of thousands of nesting penguins

42
00:04:47,628 --> 00:04:51,633
Only in a land
almost entirely covered in ice

43
00:04:51,674 --> 00:04:54,997
could bare rock
be reckoned an oasis

44
00:04:57,361 --> 00:05:01,606
Some will travel
into the heart of the continent to find it

45
00:05:03,729 --> 00:05:05,852
These are nunatak

46
00:05:05,892 --> 00:05:09,617
the exposed peaks
of vast mountain ranges

47
00:05:09,657 --> 00:05:12,941
buried in ice
over a mile deep

48
00:05:15,156 --> 00:05:17,278
The eerie
silence here

49
00:05:17,318 --> 00:05:20,121
is only
broken in spring

50
00:05:27,529 --> 00:05:30,611
The snow
petrels have arrived

51
00:05:30,652 --> 00:05:32,173
and are courting

52
00:05:39,198 --> 00:05:44,683
Antarctic petrels
now join the most southerly bird colony on Earth

53
00:05:46,326 --> 00:05:52,532
The birds have flown inland for over 300 miles to reach this breeding site

54
00:05:57,258 --> 00:05:58,820
Once their
eggs have hatched

55
00:05:58,860 --> 00:06:02,944
they will be forced repeatedly to make the 600 mile round trip

56
00:06:02,984 --> 00:06:05,266
to gather
food in the ocean

57
00:06:07,358 --> 00:06:08,358
First though

58
00:06:08,398 --> 00:06:12,838
valuable nesting
places must be defended from property thieves

59
00:06:20,197 --> 00:06:21,717
After
laying their eggs

60
00:06:21,757 --> 00:06:25,437
the petrels take
time out to clean their plumage

61
00:06:47,137 --> 00:06:52,139
The South Polar
Skua is a formidable opportunist

62
00:06:55,631 --> 00:06:59,032
But the skuas
have not chanced upon the petrels

63
00:06:59,553 --> 00:07:01,513
They've been
waiting for them

64
00:07:05,716 --> 00:07:10,598
These birds do
not need to go to the ocean for their food

65
00:07:28,405 --> 00:07:32,806
The skewers can
survive further south than any other predator

66
00:07:32,846 --> 00:07:37,486
By exploiting the
petrels' desperate need for bare rock

67
00:07:48,072 --> 00:07:49,874
Even at the
height of summer

68
00:07:49,914 --> 00:07:53,637
less than three
percent of Antarctica is free of ice

69
00:07:53,677 --> 00:07:58,199
And nearly all of
that exposed rock is found in one place

70
00:07:58,720 --> 00:08:01,041
The Antarctic Peninsula

71
00:08:07,847 --> 00:08:11,890
Its long arm extends further north than the rest of the continent

72
00:08:11,930 --> 00:08:14,892
So spring
arrives here first

73
00:08:29,577 --> 00:08:32,900
The thaw
unlocks sheltered bays

74
00:08:32,940 --> 00:08:36,184
That provide
refuge from the relentless battering

75
00:08:36,224 --> 00:08:37,985
Of the
Southern Ocean

76
00:08:42,456 --> 00:08:43,737
In the depths

77
00:08:43,777 --> 00:08:45,738
something stirs

78
00:09:10,952 --> 00:09:12,792
Humpback whales

79
00:09:12,992 --> 00:09:18,033
They have travelled over 5000 miles to reach these waters

80
00:09:24,795 --> 00:09:27,675
The whales are
harvesting krill

81
00:09:27,795 --> 00:09:30,756
Shrimplike creatures that begin to swarm here

82
00:09:30,796 --> 00:09:32,956
As soon as
the ice retreats

83
00:09:47,913 --> 00:09:50,474
Diving into the
heart of the swarm

84
00:09:50,514 --> 00:09:53,675
Teams of whales
spiral round each other

85
00:09:53,715 --> 00:09:55,916
In close
coordination

86
00:09:58,911 --> 00:10:00,271
Now they turn

87
00:10:00,311 --> 00:10:02,553
blasting air
from their blowholes

88
00:10:02,593 --> 00:10:05,154
and ascend
towards their prey

89
00:10:06,876 --> 00:10:09,037
The krill
becomes concentrated

90
00:10:09,077 --> 00:10:12,719
as the spiralling
net of bubbles draws inwards

91
00:10:40,374 --> 00:10:43,454
The team of
whales work round the clock

92
00:10:43,494 --> 00:10:45,613
for the boom
is shortlived

93
00:10:45,813 --> 00:10:47,813
Summer is
already fading

94
00:10:47,853 --> 00:10:50,453
and the whales
will soon be forced north

95
00:10:50,493 --> 00:10:52,332
as winter returns

96
00:10:56,374 --> 00:10:59,017
The sun's
influence diminishes

97
00:10:59,057 --> 00:11:01,779
and the ocean
starts to freeze

98
00:11:01,979 --> 00:11:06,383
The greatest seasonal change on our planet is underway

99
00:11:12,469 --> 00:11:15,472
The ice grows at
an extraordinary rate

100
00:11:15,512 --> 00:11:18,314
advancing two and
a half miles a day

101
00:11:18,594 --> 00:11:19,916
In a matter of weeks

102
00:11:19,956 --> 00:11:23,438
the continent
effectively doubles in size

103
00:11:27,089 --> 00:11:30,331
Life flees
from Antarctica

104
00:11:37,057 --> 00:11:40,178
But one creature
is just arriving

105
00:11:45,183 --> 00:11:46,464
Every winter

106
00:11:46,504 --> 00:11:50,227
emperor penguins
leave the comfort of their ocean home

107
00:11:50,267 --> 00:11:53,029
and begin a
remarkable journey

108
00:11:55,223 --> 00:11:57,984
They head towards
their breeding grounds

109
00:11:58,024 --> 00:12:01,144
almost a
hundred miles inland

110
00:12:24,274 --> 00:12:25,154
Eventually

111
00:12:25,194 --> 00:12:28,994
the emperor penguins reach the place where they were hatched

112
00:12:29,034 --> 00:12:33,594
An area sheltered
by icebergs trapped in the frozen ocean

113
00:12:34,874 --> 00:12:35,395
Here

114
00:12:35,435 --> 00:12:38,075
they will raise
the next generation

115
00:12:44,864 --> 00:12:45,864
But first

116
00:12:45,904 --> 00:12:51,266
each must
find a mate

117
00:12:51,306 --> 00:12:54,107
Males begin
to serenade

118
00:12:58,589 --> 00:13:00,670
And if a
female replies

119
00:13:00,710 --> 00:13:02,230
they pair up

120
00:13:02,270 --> 00:13:04,351
posing
like statues

121
00:13:08,495 --> 00:13:11,935
New couples
quickly form a strong bond

122
00:13:11,975 --> 00:13:15,935
They seem oblivious to the noisy crowd around them

123
00:13:18,375 --> 00:13:20,135
To cement
their relationship

124
00:13:20,175 --> 00:13:23,415
the male steps
out with his female

125
00:13:33,524 --> 00:13:35,569
The brief
courtship complete

126
00:13:35,609 --> 00:13:38,175
There isn't a
moment to lose

127
00:13:38,297 --> 00:13:40,503
With so much
pressure to perform

128
00:13:40,543 --> 00:13:44,111
Any male would
struggle to stay on top

129
00:13:51,876 --> 00:13:53,435
Several
weeks later

130
00:13:53,475 --> 00:13:56,995
and it seems that
most couplings were successful

131
00:13:57,954 --> 00:14:01,793
But producing the
egg has taken its toll

132
00:14:02,273 --> 00:14:06,072
The females no
longer have the energy to incubate

133
00:14:08,142 --> 00:14:10,262
The male
takes over

134
00:14:10,302 --> 00:14:13,341
It's still minus
20 degrees centigrade

135
00:14:13,381 --> 00:14:15,581
So the transfer
must be done quickly

136
00:14:15,621 --> 00:14:17,621
or else the
egg will freeze

137
00:14:17,901 --> 00:14:20,061
With no bare
rock to nest on

138
00:14:20,101 --> 00:14:23,221
The male tucks
the egg into a special pouch

139
00:14:23,261 --> 00:14:25,221
Where he can
keep it warm

140
00:14:26,181 --> 00:14:29,861
It requires an
extraordinary piece of teamwork

141
00:14:34,214 --> 00:14:35,694
Driven by hunger

142
00:14:35,734 --> 00:14:40,176
the exhausted females now return to the ocean on their own

143
00:14:40,216 --> 00:14:45,457
repeating the epic journey they made with the males only a month before

144
00:14:51,700 --> 00:14:55,181
Now the sun
barely appears above the horizon

145
00:14:56,301 --> 00:14:57,702
As the
days shorten

146
00:14:57,742 --> 00:15:01,103
its warmth is
withdrawn from the continent

147
00:15:12,661 --> 00:15:14,104
With the
females gone

148
00:15:14,144 --> 00:15:18,311
the colony undergoes a strange transformation

149
00:15:19,234 --> 00:15:21,679
The males
shuffle into groups

150
00:15:21,719 --> 00:15:25,364
their eggs still
tucked away above their feet

151
00:15:28,009 --> 00:15:31,210
They lock together in tightly packed huddles

152
00:15:31,250 --> 00:15:33,610
as they
struggle to keep warm

153
00:15:37,652 --> 00:15:42,973
Speeding up the action reveals how these huddles constantly shift and change

154
00:15:43,013 --> 00:15:47,815
As each penguin
works its way towards the warmer interior

155
00:15:49,128 --> 00:15:50,772
Crammed
into this scrum

156
00:15:50,812 --> 00:15:53,459
the birds are
remarkably good natured

157
00:15:53,499 --> 00:15:55,061
But they
have to be

158
00:15:55,343 --> 00:15:56,786
If the
huddle breaks

159
00:15:56,826 --> 00:15:58,310
even for a moment

160
00:15:58,350 --> 00:16:00,674
precious
heat escapes

161
00:16:19,191 --> 00:16:23,317
It's imperative
they reform as quickly as possible

162
00:16:23,357 --> 00:16:25,440
For only by
acting as one

163
00:16:25,480 --> 00:16:27,683
Can the males
withstand the elements

164
00:16:27,723 --> 00:16:29,685
And protect
their eggs

165
00:16:35,157 --> 00:16:38,678
But their
greatest test lies ahead

166
00:16:39,199 --> 00:16:40,920
As winter advances

167
00:16:40,960 --> 00:16:44,482
frequent blizzards drive the temperature down

168
00:16:45,283 --> 00:16:49,084
It's now 60
degrees below zero

169
00:16:52,006 --> 00:16:54,448
The birds at the
edge of the huddle

170
00:16:54,488 --> 00:16:57,529
Bear the brunt of
the hundred mile an hour winds

171
00:16:57,610 --> 00:17:02,172
And so provide shelter to those taking their turn in the middle

172
00:17:11,071 --> 00:17:12,872
Abandoned
by the sun

173
00:17:12,912 --> 00:17:15,233
The males are
left alone with their eggs

174
00:17:15,273 --> 00:17:16,794
To face
the coldest

175
00:17:16,834 --> 00:17:19,434
darkest
winter on Earth

176
00:17:27,357 --> 00:17:30,239
At the northern
extreme of our planet

177
00:17:30,279 --> 00:17:33,760
the sun rises for
the first time in months

178
00:17:33,800 --> 00:17:37,402
illuminating a
very different ice world

179
00:17:40,563 --> 00:17:42,444
Unlike Antarctica

180
00:17:42,484 --> 00:17:45,805
the Arctic is a
vast frozen sea

181
00:17:45,966 --> 00:17:47,966
surrounded by land

182
00:17:49,744 --> 00:17:50,505
Here

183
00:17:50,545 --> 00:17:52,985
winter is
coming to an end

184
00:17:53,266 --> 00:17:57,428
But this bleak
wilderness remains locked in ice

185
00:17:57,468 --> 00:18:08,793
Eider ducks
break the silence

186
00:18:08,833 --> 00:18:12,274
They have stayed
here braving the northern winter

187
00:18:12,314 --> 00:18:15,796
Instead of flying
south to warmer climes

188
00:18:19,548 --> 00:18:21,110
Flocks

189
00:18:21,150 --> 00:18:22,111
40，000 strong

190
00:18:22,151 --> 00:18:24,993
sweep across
the frozen wastes

191
00:18:38,783 --> 00:18:41,304
They all have
the same goal

192
00:18:41,465 --> 00:18:42,545
A polynya

193
00:18:42,585 --> 00:18:44,827
a permanent
hole in the sea ice

194
00:18:44,867 --> 00:18:49,229
Kept open throughout the winter by strong ocean currents

195
00:18:53,272 --> 00:18:58,116
This unusual duck
pond provides an overnight sanctuary

196
00:18:58,156 --> 00:18:59,917
And when
day breaks

197
00:18:59,957 --> 00:19:02,318
a rare
chance to feed

198
00:19:14,874 --> 00:19:17,275
Just ten metres
beneath the ice

199
00:19:17,315 --> 00:19:21,677
the sea floor is
carpeted with dense mussel beds

200
00:19:23,359 --> 00:19:27,520
These can only be
reached during a brief lull in the currents

201
00:19:27,641 --> 00:19:31,003
The ducks must
quickly prize the mussels free

202
00:19:31,043 --> 00:19:33,804
before the tide
starts to turn

203
00:19:40,430 --> 00:19:43,352
The window of
opportunity is short

204
00:19:43,432 --> 00:19:45,474
As the current
begins to build

205
00:19:45,514 --> 00:19:46,955
it's up，up

206
00:19:46,995 --> 00:19:48,196
and away

207
00:19:59,925 --> 00:20:02,687
These permanent
holes in the ice

208
00:20:02,727 --> 00:20:05,769
provide seafood
throughout the winter

209
00:20:07,562 --> 00:20:10,044
The diners
attract others

210
00:20:16,769 --> 00:20:17,889
In the Arctic

211
00:20:17,929 --> 00:20:20,411
any breach in
the icy barrier

212
00:20:20,451 --> 00:20:22,172
can be a lifeline

213
00:20:26,135 --> 00:20:27,856
Muskrats can
create their own

214
00:20:29,962 --> 00:20:34,403
These giants have
the strength to smash through the frozen crust

215
00:20:34,443 --> 00:20:37,163
to graze on the
vegetation below

216
00:20:41,564 --> 00:20:46,205
These icebreakers
create an opening for other overwinterers

217
00:20:47,686 --> 00:20:49,166
Flocks of
ptarmigan

218
00:20:49,206 --> 00:20:53,007
make unusual
grazing companions to the muskoxen

219
00:20:53,207 --> 00:20:56,288
Whose entourage
grows throughout the day

220
00:21:05,228 --> 00:21:07,912
This odd
assembly of vegetarians

221
00:21:07,952 --> 00:21:10,194
doesn't
go unnoticed

222
00:21:11,396 --> 00:21:13,078
An Arctic fox

223
00:21:18,646 --> 00:21:21,729
The muskoxen have
recently given birth

224
00:21:22,892 --> 00:21:23,893
For the fox

225
00:21:23,933 --> 00:21:26,335
it's a
chance to scavenge

226
00:21:33,365 --> 00:21:36,365
But half a ton
of mad hairy cow

227
00:21:37,045 --> 00:21:39,245
is not to be
trifled with

228
00:21:48,444 --> 00:21:51,603
The calves are
born well before the spring melt

229
00:21:51,643 --> 00:21:56,243
Giving them a
head start when summer finally arrives

230
00:22:00,277 --> 00:22:03,917
It must get to
grips with its new ice world

231
00:22:04,276 --> 00:22:05,956
Benign one minute

232
00:22:05,996 --> 00:22:08,196
lifethreatening the next

233
00:22:14,396 --> 00:22:15,876
Even in spring

234
00:22:15,916 --> 00:22:21,196
winds chill
to the bone

235
00:22:21,396 --> 00:22:24,076
The calf must
stay close to its mother

236
00:22:24,116 --> 00:22:27,316
To avoid getting
lost in the sudden blizzard

237
00:22:37,138 --> 00:22:38,938
Arctic wolves

238
00:22:43,141 --> 00:22:44,342
In the whiteout

239
00:22:44,382 --> 00:22:47,304
the threat is
almost impossible to detect

240
00:22:47,344 --> 00:22:51,346
But the muskoxen
instinctively retreat to higher ground

241
00:23:07,800 --> 00:23:10,721
Forming a defensive ring around their calves

242
00:23:10,761 --> 00:23:14,923
The adults present a barricade that few hunters could breach

243
00:23:17,525 --> 00:23:21,326
But the wolves
need not risk injury today

244
00:23:23,288 --> 00:23:26,889
A calf has been
left behind in the panic

245
00:23:40,126 --> 00:23:42,086
With each
passing day

246
00:23:42,126 --> 00:23:44,846
the sun climbs
higher in the sky

247
00:23:44,886 --> 00:23:48,406
and its rays
strike the Arctic more directly

248
00:23:49,566 --> 00:23:50,926
It's spring

249
00:23:50,966 --> 00:23:53,726
and new life stirs

250
00:23:58,142 --> 00:24:02,585
The polar bear cubs emerge from the den in which they were born

251
00:24:02,866 --> 00:24:08,389
Their mother stretches her legs after five months under the snow

252
00:24:13,392 --> 00:24:15,674
They're just
two months old

253
00:24:15,714 --> 00:24:18,636
and instinctively follow her lead

254
00:24:34,652 --> 00:24:38,296
A steep slope
makes the best site for a den

255
00:24:38,336 --> 00:24:42,299
But it's a tricky
place to take your first steps

256
00:25:01,719 --> 00:25:03,400
It may
look like fun

257
00:25:03,440 --> 00:25:07,280
But this is
serious training for the task ahead

258
00:25:07,441 --> 00:25:09,361
There's no
food on the slopes

259
00:25:09,401 --> 00:25:12,802
And the family
will need to head out across the frozen sea

260
00:25:12,842 --> 00:25:15,483
Before their
mother's milk runs dry

261
00:25:22,525 --> 00:25:24,085
Two weeks later

262
00:25:24,286 --> 00:25:25,566
they're ready

263
00:25:26,867 --> 00:25:28,309
Out on the sea ice

264
00:25:28,349 --> 00:25:30,791
the female can
hunt for seals

265
00:25:31,032 --> 00:25:33,594
But it will take
all her mothering skills

266
00:25:33,634 --> 00:25:35,396
to keep
her cubs safe

267
00:25:35,436 --> 00:25:38,319
in this
dangerous world of ice

268
00:25:53,743 --> 00:25:56,706
The annual
melt has begun

269
00:25:58,830 --> 00:26:02,675
This is a challenging time for the bear family

270
00:26:02,916 --> 00:26:07,162
One out of every
two cubs do not survive their first year

271
00:26:07,202 --> 00:26:08,683
out on the ice

272
00:26:19,787 --> 00:26:22,068
As the sun's
influence increases

273
00:26:22,108 --> 00:26:25,670
the sea ice seems
to take on a life of its own

274
00:26:34,195 --> 00:26:37,397
Glacial meltwaters pour from the land

275
00:26:37,437 --> 00:26:40,959
mingling with the
sea and speeding up the thaw

276
00:26:46,335 --> 00:26:49,416
The seascape is
in constant flux

277
00:26:49,456 --> 00:26:53,456
as broken ice is
moved on by winds and currents

278
00:27:02,858 --> 00:27:05,299
The ice is
becoming too weak

279
00:27:05,339 --> 00:27:08,219
to support a
male polar bear

280
00:27:16,357 --> 00:27:19,197
He attempts to
spread his weight

281
00:27:19,237 --> 00:27:22,038
But the ice that
has supported him all winter

282
00:27:22,078 --> 00:27:24,398
Is rapidly
disintegrating

283
00:27:26,518 --> 00:27:27,479
Each year

284
00:27:27,519 --> 00:27:28,919
as the
climate warms

285
00:27:28,959 --> 00:27:31,599
The Arctic
holds less ice

286
00:27:31,679 --> 00:27:34,840
This is a
disaster for polar bears

287
00:27:34,880 --> 00:27:36,840
Without its
solid platform

288
00:27:36,880 --> 00:27:39,161
They can't hunt
the seals they need

289
00:27:39,201 --> 00:27:41,041
In order
to survive

290
00:27:44,404 --> 00:27:47,883
This may be a
glimpse of the unstable future

291
00:27:47,923 --> 00:27:51,083
faced by this
magnificent creature

292
00:28:24,870 --> 00:28:26,872
As the ice
disappears

293
00:28:26,912 --> 00:28:30,435
sea birds return
to the high Arctic

294
00:28:38,837 --> 00:28:42,201
Little auks
arrive in their millions

295
00:28:44,885 --> 00:28:45,887
In some ways

296
00:28:45,927 --> 00:28:49,291
these birds are
the penguins of the north

297
00:28:49,331 --> 00:28:52,215
They seek bare
rock on which to lay their eggs

298
00:28:52,255 --> 00:28:54,778
And they look
rather like penguins too

299
00:29:01,736 --> 00:29:03,217
Unlike Antarctica

300
00:29:03,257 --> 00:29:06,739
the Arctic can
be reached by landbased predators

301
00:29:06,779 --> 00:29:10,420
which is why little orcs have kept the ability to fly

302
00:29:15,984 --> 00:29:19,265
They use screeslopes to protect their eggs

303
00:29:19,305 --> 00:29:22,147
burrowing up to a
metre beneath the rocks

304
00:29:29,354 --> 00:29:30,875
At the
height of summer

305
00:29:30,915 --> 00:29:32,716
the sun never sets

306
00:29:32,756 --> 00:29:34,917
but just
skims the horizon

307
00:29:34,957 --> 00:29:36,797
before
rising again

308
00:29:42,360 --> 00:29:44,762
Migrants
return to the Arctic

309
00:29:44,802 --> 00:29:46,842
from far and wide

310
00:29:47,523 --> 00:29:51,004
They've come to
make the most of the brief flush of food

311
00:29:51,044 --> 00:29:52,965
and to
produce their young

312
00:29:54,802 --> 00:29:59,481
Sandhill cranes
have travelled all the way from New Mexico

313
00:30:05,159 --> 00:30:08,838
Their chicks join
the growing band of youngsters

314
00:30:08,878 --> 00:30:10,838
Exploring
the tundra

315
00:30:18,005 --> 00:30:20,007
For a few
months each year

316
00:30:20,047 --> 00:30:23,971
the Arctic becomes the land of the midnight sun

317
00:30:24,172 --> 00:30:26,534
and 24 hours
of daylight

318
00:30:26,574 --> 00:30:29,657
allow animals to
feed around the clock

319
00:30:31,100 --> 00:30:34,303
The Arctic fox
finally has enough food

320
00:30:34,343 --> 00:30:36,665
to raise her
large family

321
00:30:52,305 --> 00:30:54,508
If you choose to
nest in the open

322
00:30:54,548 --> 00:30:57,111
You must be
prepared for a fight

323
00:31:06,364 --> 00:31:09,848
Arctic skewers
will see off any trespassers

324
00:31:09,888 --> 00:31:12,209
even large
vegetarians

325
00:31:58,569 --> 00:32:04,497
The male polar bear's ice world has finally vanished beneath him

326
00:32:16,400 --> 00:32:21,359
While the female
is still kept on land by her dependent cubs

327
00:32:21,399 --> 00:32:24,878
The male can take
to the sea in search of food

328
00:32:25,238 --> 00:32:26,678
Ducking and diving

329
00:32:26,718 --> 00:32:28,958
He hopes to
ambush seals

330
00:32:28,998 --> 00:32:32,077
Resting on the
remaining fragments of ice

331
00:32:59,560 --> 00:33:01,840
In these new
surroundings

332
00:33:01,880 --> 00:33:05,361
he is a surprisingly adept swimmer

333
00:33:16,031 --> 00:33:18,873
Once an
extremely rare sight

334
00:33:18,913 --> 00:33:24,035
polar bears have
recently been seen over 60 miles from the shore

335
00:33:38,085 --> 00:33:42,047
There is now no
turning back for this bear

336
00:33:42,247 --> 00:33:46,169
He's forced to
head out into deeper water

337
00:33:53,975 --> 00:33:59,138
His giant front
paws help him to fight the ocean currents

338
00:34:20,246 --> 00:34:22,810
He seems at
home in the sea

339
00:34:22,931 --> 00:34:25,976
But he cannot
swim indefinitely

340
00:34:29,144 --> 00:34:30,466
He will drown

341
00:34:30,506 --> 00:34:32,751
if he doesn't
find land somewhere

342
00:34:32,791 --> 00:34:34,433
In this vast ocean

343
00:35:02,897 --> 00:35:07,179
Walruses are now
gathering on low lying islands

344
00:35:07,459 --> 00:35:09,940
They gave
birth on sea ice

345
00:35:09,980 --> 00:35:11,981
But with this
platform now gone

346
00:35:12,021 --> 00:35:16,103
They need a new
place to haul out and nurse their young

347
00:35:18,639 --> 00:35:20,720
After several days at sea

348
00:35:20,800 --> 00:35:23,841
the male bear
finally makes landfall

349
00:35:23,881 --> 00:35:28,562
drawn by pungent
smells emanating from the island

350
00:35:36,831 --> 00:35:38,474
By the end
of summer

351
00:35:38,514 --> 00:35:41,198
the bear has
lost half his weight

352
00:35:41,520 --> 00:35:43,284
With the
ice long gone

353
00:35:43,324 --> 00:35:47,010
he is forced onto
land in search of food

354
00:35:56,269 --> 00:36:00,070
There will be no
easy meals on this island

355
00:36:00,391 --> 00:36:03,432
Walruses are the
largest seals in the world

356
00:36:03,472 --> 00:36:05,313
They weigh
over a ton

357
00:36:05,353 --> 00:36:08,515
and are armed
with tusks a metre long

358
00:36:11,717 --> 00:36:13,678
Exhausted
from his swim

359
00:36:13,718 --> 00:36:16,559
the bear must
regain his strength

360
00:36:23,500 --> 00:36:24,580
The next day

361
00:36:24,620 --> 00:36:27,580
a sea fog
shrouds the island

362
00:36:27,740 --> 00:36:30,859
The walruses
sense that they are in danger

363
00:36:34,099 --> 00:36:35,898
Using the
fog as cover

364
00:36:35,938 --> 00:36:38,498
the bear
approaches the herd

365
00:36:43,579 --> 00:36:46,424
The adults close
ranks around their young

366
00:36:46,464 --> 00:36:49,869
presenting a wall
of blubber and hide

367
00:36:57,601 --> 00:36:59,881
He tests
the barrier

368
00:37:00,522 --> 00:37:02,322
but it stands firm

369
00:37:03,762 --> 00:37:07,523
It appears that
the world's largest land carnivore

370
00:37:07,563 --> 00:37:09,163
has met his match

371
00:37:16,965 --> 00:37:19,885
There must be a
chink in the armour somewhere

372
00:37:23,996 --> 00:37:25,197
Not here

373
00:37:34,327 --> 00:37:37,491
This female
walrus is shielding her pup

374
00:37:37,531 --> 00:37:39,973
If he can just
prise her off

375
00:37:47,263 --> 00:37:51,908
The bear's claws
and teeth can't penetrate her thick hide

376
00:38:00,639 --> 00:38:02,841
With the herd
retreating to water

377
00:38:02,881 --> 00:38:05,163
the bear must
move quickly

378
00:38:10,489 --> 00:38:11,930
Having
failed with one

379
00:38:11,970 --> 00:38:14,332
he heads
straight for another

380
00:38:32,127 --> 00:38:36,345
The chance of his
first meal in months is slipping away

381
00:38:43,788 --> 00:38:46,708
He seems
increasingly desperate

382
00:38:51,868 --> 00:38:54,228
It's now or never

383
00:38:55,148 --> 00:38:58,988
He must avoid the
stabbing tusks if he's to win

384
00:39:06,162 --> 00:39:09,364
The flailing
walrus is immensely powerful

385
00:39:09,404 --> 00:39:12,045
And drags the
bear away from the shallows

386
00:39:12,085 --> 00:39:14,406
Towards the
safety of the herd

387
00:39:30,333 --> 00:39:32,653
It slips
from his grasp

388
00:39:41,260 --> 00:39:43,461
Only at the
height of summer

389
00:39:43,501 --> 00:39:46,184
when bears are on
the verge of starvation

390
00:39:46,224 --> 00:39:49,706
Will they risk
attacking such dangerous prey

391
00:39:53,910 --> 00:39:56,753
It was a gamble
that this bear took

392
00:39:56,793 --> 00:39:58,073
and lost

393
00:40:01,145 --> 00:40:04,546
The stab wounds
he received from the walrus

394
00:40:04,586 --> 00:40:06,107
are so severe

395
00:40:06,187 --> 00:40:08,147
that he can
barely walk

396
00:40:12,740 --> 00:40:15,460
The walruses
are calm again

397
00:40:15,700 --> 00:40:18,340
Seemingly aware
that the injured bear

398
00:40:18,380 --> 00:40:22,099
no longer poses a
threat to them or their young

399
00:40:38,100 --> 00:40:39,661
Unable to feed

400
00:40:39,701 --> 00:40:42,621
this bear
will not survive

401
00:40:55,381 --> 00:40:58,660
If the global
climate continues to warm

402
00:40:58,700 --> 00:41:01,900
and the Arctic
ice melts sooner each year

403
00:41:01,940 --> 00:41:05,899
it's certain that
more bears will share this fate

404
00:41:28,415 --> 00:41:31,014
At the southern
end of our planet

405
00:41:31,054 --> 00:41:34,894
Fiery ribbons are
illuminating the winter skies

406
00:41:35,254 --> 00:41:37,773
The aurora
australis

407
00:41:41,333 --> 00:41:43,772
This light
brings no warmth

408
00:41:43,812 --> 00:41:45,492
To the
male penguins

409
00:41:45,532 --> 00:41:47,172
Who are
still huddling

410
00:41:47,212 --> 00:41:50,731
Defying the
coldest conditions on the planet

411
00:42:04,747 --> 00:42:16,471
Their ordeal is
drawing to a close

412
00:42:16,511 --> 00:42:18,752
30 days
after it last set

413
00:42:18,792 --> 00:42:22,953
the sun rises
once more on Antarctica

414
00:42:45,411 --> 00:42:50,056
Their appalling
trials have all been for this

415
00:43:06,253 --> 00:43:10,895
Each father has
just one meal left inside him

416
00:43:11,456 --> 00:43:14,377
He's been
saving it all winter

417
00:43:15,698 --> 00:43:20,700
This single feed
will sustain the chicks for a few more days

418
00:43:21,579 --> 00:43:24,699
But the males
have not eaten for nearly four months

419
00:43:24,739 --> 00:43:26,660
If they do
not eat soon

420
00:43:26,700 --> 00:43:29,621
they and their
chicks will die

421
00:43:39,624 --> 00:43:41,064
But there is hope

422
00:43:41,104 --> 00:43:42,585
on the horizon

423
00:43:55,119 --> 00:43:57,561
The females
are returning

424
00:43:57,601 --> 00:44:00,763
and their bellies
are full with fish

425
00:44:07,129 --> 00:44:08,450
As they approach

426
00:44:08,490 --> 00:44:12,052
waves of excitement ripple through the huddle

427
00:44:20,229 --> 00:44:23,072
Each female
calls to her mate

428
00:44:23,473 --> 00:44:24,354
And he

429
00:44:24,394 --> 00:44:26,316
recognising her song

430
00:44:26,356 --> 00:44:27,997
trumpets back

431
00:44:30,441 --> 00:44:33,123
Reunited，at last

432
00:44:36,328 --> 00:44:39,932
The mother sees
her chick for the first time

433
00:44:44,076 --> 00:44:46,879
She's keen to
start parenting

434
00:44:47,240 --> 00:44:48,962
But the father
needs persuading

435
00:44:49,002 --> 00:44:52,646
To surrender the
chick he's been caring for all winter

436
00:44:57,653 --> 00:45:00,417
He must now put
his chick at risk

437
00:45:00,457 --> 00:45:03,820
In these temperatures it could freeze in seconds

438
00:45:07,853 --> 00:45:10,336
The male will
have to let go

439
00:45:17,386 --> 00:45:18,347
Eventually

440
00:45:18,387 --> 00:45:21,671
the transfer to
the mother is safely made

441
00:45:36,657 --> 00:45:41,419
The chicks grow
quickly on a diet of fish and squid

442
00:45:55,545 --> 00:45:56,186
Soon

443
00:45:56,226 --> 00:45:58,267
they're
keen to explore

444
00:45:58,307 --> 00:46:01,027
but always
with mother in tow

445
00:46:03,376 --> 00:46:05,819
This chick is
less fortunate

446
00:46:05,859 --> 00:46:09,463
Its mother has
not returned to claim it

447
00:46:11,386 --> 00:46:14,469
Another orphan is
searching for a new family

448
00:46:14,509 --> 00:46:18,073
But this female
already has a chick of her own

449
00:46:26,815 --> 00:46:30,575
Some orphans receive too much mothering from penguins

450
00:46:30,615 --> 00:46:33,094
whose own chicks
have not survived

451
00:46:33,134 --> 00:46:35,414
The urge to
parent is so strong

452
00:46:35,454 --> 00:46:37,453
that they will
compete with one another

453
00:46:37,493 --> 00:46:39,933
to adopt any
chick they find

454
00:46:45,492 --> 00:46:48,572
Many of these
squabbles end in tragedy

455
00:46:48,612 --> 00:46:51,811
as the poor chick
is trampled to death

456
00:47:04,008 --> 00:47:09,370
Those chicks that
do have parents quickly learn survival skills

457
00:47:11,011 --> 00:47:12,292
Even in spring

458
00:47:12,332 --> 00:47:14,292
they must huddle
together for warmth

459
00:47:14,332 --> 00:47:17,654
just as their
fathers did in the depths of winter

460
00:47:21,896 --> 00:47:25,217
A group of chicks
has got lost in the blizzard

461
00:47:32,246 --> 00:47:34,366
Cold and
disorientated

462
00:47:34,406 --> 00:47:36,526
they search
for the colony

463
00:47:43,127 --> 00:47:44,607
It will
not be long

464
00:47:44,647 --> 00:47:48,447
before the storm
claims its first victims

465
00:48:07,239 --> 00:48:08,640
By early summer

466
00:48:08,680 --> 00:48:11,881
the chicks are
surprisingly well developed

467
00:48:12,402 --> 00:48:15,604
And now look
ready to take on the world

468
00:48:17,645 --> 00:48:20,007
Those that
survive their first year

469
00:48:20,047 --> 00:48:22,529
have the best
possible start in life

470
00:48:22,569 --> 00:48:27,531
Thanks to the extraordinary hardships endured by their parents

471
00:48:28,372 --> 00:48:32,455
Parents who
battled with the Antarctic winter

472
00:48:32,495 --> 00:48:33,655
and won

473
00:48:43,576 --> 00:48:44,817
In the Arctic

474
00:48:44,857 --> 00:48:46,818
the two
polar bear cubs

475
00:48:46,858 --> 00:48:49,100
are now independent of their mother

476
00:48:49,140 --> 00:48:50,982
and they
briefly reunite

477
00:48:51,022 --> 00:48:53,543
where their
home ranges overlap

478
00:49:00,661 --> 00:49:03,663
Their time
together will be fleeting

479
00:49:04,024 --> 00:49:06,987
Most of their
lives are now spent alone

480
00:49:07,108 --> 00:49:10,471
Wandering the
vast tracts of frozen ocean

481
00:49:40,203 --> 00:49:41,764
Following
their mother

482
00:49:41,804 --> 00:49:45,006
has prepared
them for life at the pole

483
00:49:45,086 --> 00:49:47,127
An ever
changing land

484
00:49:47,288 --> 00:49:49,249
ruled by ice

485
00:49:51,267 --> 00:49:53,466
Whether they are
ready for the bigger changes

486
00:49:53,506 --> 00:49:56,866
that have begun
to shape the ice worlds of our planet

487
00:49:56,906 --> 00:49:59,026
remains to be seen

